Comment (cmd/agent.go:1105):
Blocking (R33: "SIGTERM keeps its 30s"): during a reload drain, this branch waits up to `reloadDrainTimeout` (5m) without reading `sigs`. `signal.Notify` has disabled the default SIGTERM action. After the drain, `defer signal.Stop(sigs)` discards the buffered signal. So a SIGTERM (`systemctl stop`, a pod eviction) that arrives while a reload is draining is **lost**, not delayed. The agent keeps running on the new config until SIGKILL (systemd `TimeoutStopSec`, the k8s grace period), and plugin children may be left behind.

This was already latent on `main` with the 30s drain. The 5-minute drain turns it into a realistic window.

Suggested fix: pass `sigs` into the drain wait, e.g. a `select` over the cron stop, `time.After(reloadDrainTimeout)` and `<-sigs`. On a signal, switch to the SIGTERM path: `daemonCronStopTimeout`, `closePluginClients`, `os.Exit(0)`. A small test with an injected signal channel would pin it.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Fix: the reload drain select also reads sigs (waitForDrain helper returning the signal). On a signal: wait the remaining daemonCronStopTimeout (30s), closePluginClients, exit(0). Add seams for signal registration and exit so a test can inject SIGTERM mid-drain and assert exit(0) happens promptly.

